<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-radical" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Radical of a module</title>
    
    
    <introduction>
        <p>
            We assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity, and that <m>A</m> is a right <m>R</m>-algebra. Consider <m>M</m> as a right <m>A</m>-module.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <definition xml:id="def-radical-of-a-module">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <term>radical</term> of a right <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is
                <me>
                    \rad{M}=\bigcap_{\substack{N\leq M\\M/N\text{ is simple}}}N
                </me>
                If <m>M</m> has no <m>A</m>-submodule <m>N</m> such that <m>M/N</m> is simple then, we write <m>\rad{M}=M</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <example xml:id="ex-radical-is-whole-module">
        <title>Radical of <m>\Q</m> as a <m>\Z</m>-module is <m>\Q</m></title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider <m>\Q</m> as a <m>\Z</m>-module. Suppose there is a proper submodule <m>N</m> of <m>\Q</m> such that <m>\Q/N</m> is simple. By <xref ref="prop-equivalent-criterion-for-simple-modules"/>, there exists a prime number <m>p</m> such that <m>\Q/N\simeq\Z/p\Z</m> as <m>\Z</m>-modules. Thus, for any <m>x\in\Q</m>, <m>px\in N</m>. Choose <m>x\in\Q\setminus N</m>. Then there is <m>y\in\Q</m> such that <m>py=x</m>. Note that <m>x=py\in N</m>, a contradiction. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
    <p>
        We collect a few observations related to radical in the following lemma.
    </p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-observations-related-to-radical">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>M</m> be a right <m>A</m>-module.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The radical of <m>M</m> is an <m>A</m>-submodule of <m>M</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>N\leq M</m> and <m>\rad{(M/N)}=0</m> then, <m>\rad{M}\subseteq N</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The radical of <m>M/\rad{M}</m> is zero.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-radical-of-semisimple-module-is-trivial">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If a right <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is semisimple then, <m>\rad{M}=0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Let <m>M=\oplus_{i\in I}N_i</m>, where <m>N_i</m> is a simple <m>A</m>-module. For every <m>i\in I</m> consider <m>L_i=\oplus_{k\neq i}N_k</m>. Then, <m>M/L_i\simeq N_i</m> is simple for each <m>i\in I</m>. Thus, <m>\rad{M}\subseteq\cap_iL_i=\{0\}</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-artinian-and-radical-zero-iff-finitely-generated-semisimple">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A right <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is finitely generated and semisimple if and only if <m>M</m> is Artinian and <m>\rad{M}=0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>M</m> is a finitely generated and semisimple <m>A</m>-module. By <xref ref="thm-semisimple-artinian-noetherian-modules"/> and <xref ref="lem-radical-of-semisimple-module-is-trivial"/>, the module <m>M</m> is Artinian and its radical is trivial.
        </p>
        <p>
            Conversely, assume that <m>M</m> is Artinian and that <m>\rad{M}=0</m>. Suppose that <m>\rad{M}</m> is the inversection of <m>N_i</m> (<m>i\in I</m>) such that <m>M/N_i</m> is simple. Note that as <m>\rad{M}=0</m>, the set <m>I</m> is not empty. Consider the following collection of <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m>
            <me>
                \Sigma=\{N_{i_1}\cap\cdots\cap N_{i_k}:i_1,\ldots,i_k\in I\}
            </me>
            This is a nonempty collection of submodules of an Artinian module, hence, by <xref ref="lem-existence-of-minimal-maximal-element"/>, the collection <m>\Sigma</m> has a minimal element, say <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r</m>. We first observe that this minimal element is zero. Indeed, if <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r\neq 0</m> then for some <m>i\in I</m>, <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r</m> is not a subset of <m>N_i</m> and hence, <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r\cap N_i\subsetneq N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r</m>, a contradiction to minimality. Therefore, <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r=0</m>. We define an <m>A</m>-module homomorphism <m>\varphi\colon M\to\oplus_{i=1}^rM/N_i</m> by 
            <me>
                m\mapsto(m+N_1,\ldots,m+N_r).
            </me>
            This is a surjective map with the kernel <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r</m>. Hence, 
            <me>
                M\simeq (M/N_1)\oplus\cdots\oplus(M/N_r).
            </me>
            This shows that <m>M</m> is semisimple. It is also finitely generated because <m>M</m> given to be Artinian (see <xref ref="thm-semisimple-artinian-noetherian-modules"/>).
        </p>
    </proof>
    <definition xml:id="def-socle-of-module">
        <title>Socle of a module</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>M</m> be a right <m>A</m>-module. The <term>socle</term> of <m>M</m>, denoted by <m>\soc{M}</m>, is defined as 
                <me>
                    \soc{M}=\sum\left\{N\leq M:N\text{ is simple}\right\}.
                </me>
                 If <m>M</m> has no simple <m>A</m>-submodule then, we write <m>\soc{M}=0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <note xml:id="note-socle-is-semisimple">
        <title>Socle is semisimple</title>
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                By <xref ref="cor-sum-of-simple-modules-is-direct-sum"/>, it follows that <m>\soc{M}</m> is a semisimple <m>A</m>-module. Furthermore, <m>M</m> is semisimple if and only if <m>\soc{M}=M</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </note>
    <definition xml:id="def-essential-submodule">
        <title>Essential submodule</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                An <m>A</m>-submodule <m>N</m> of a right <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is said to be <term>essential</term> if for every nonzero <m>A</m>-submodule <m>L</m> of <m>M</m>, <m>N\cap L\neq 0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <note xml:id="note-essential-submodule-is-nonzero">
        <statement>
            <p>
                An essential submodule is always nonzero.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </note>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-socle-as-intersection-of-essential-submodules">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The socle of a right <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is the intersection of all essential <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m>.
                <me>
                    \soc{M}=\bigcap_{\substack{E\leq M\\E\text{ is essential}}}E
                </me>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <proof>
        <p>
            For a simple submodule <m>N\leq M</m> and a nonzero <m>u\in N</m>, we have <m>N=uA</m>. Thus, if <m>E\leq M</m> is an essential submodule then, <m>N\cap E\neq 0</m> and hence, <m>N\subseteq E</m>. This shows that <m>\soc{M}</m> is contained in every essential submodule of <m>M</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Conversely, let <m>S</m> be the intersection of all essential <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m>. We show that <m>S</m> is semisimple by showing that any submodule of <m>S</m> has a complement in <m>S</m>. Let <m>N\leq S</m>. Consider the following collection of submodules of <m>M</m>.
            <me>
                \Sigma=\{N'\leq M:N'\cap N=0\}
            </me>
            As <m>0\in\Sigma</m>, the collection <m>\Sigma\neq\emptyset</m>. Suppose that <m>\{N_i'\}</m> is a chain in <m>\Sigma</m>. We show that <m>\cup N_i'\in\Sigma</m>. Indeed, if <m>x\in(\cup N_i')\cap N</m> then, <m>x\in N_j'</m> for some <m>j</m>. Thus, <m>x=0</m> as <m>N_j'\in\Sigma</m>. By Zorn's lemma, there exists a maximal element <m>N'</m> in <m>\Sigma</m>. We claim that <m>N+N'</m> is an essential submodule of <m>M</m>. Suppose not, then there exists a nonzero submodule <m>L\leq M</m> such that <m>(N+N')\cap L=0</m>. If there exists <m>x\in N\cap (L+N')</m> then, <m>x=\ell+n'</m> for <m>\ell\in L</m> and <m>n'\in N'</m>. Thus, <m>\ell=x-n'\in L\cap (N+N')</m>. This shows that <m>N\cap(L+N')=0</m> and hence, <m>N'+L\in\Sigma</m>, contradicting the maximality of <m>N'</m>. Thus, <m>N+N'</m> is an essential submodule of <m>M</m> and hence, it contains <m>S</m>. This shows that <m>S=N+(N'\cap S)</m>. As <m>N+N'=N\oplus N'</m> we have <m>S=N\oplus(N'\cap S)</m>. Therefore, every submodule of <m>S</m> has a complement in <m>S</m> and hence, <m>S</m> is semisimple (see <xref ref="cor-semisimple-implies-existence-of-complement"/>). This shows that <m>S\subseteq\soc{M}</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-socle-of-artinian-module-is-essential">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>M</m> is an Artinian right <m>A</m>-module then, <m>\soc{M}</m> is an essential <m>A</m>-submodule of <m>M</m>. In particular, <m>\soc{M}\neq 0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Let <m>N\leq M</m> be a nonzero <m>A</m>-submodule. As <m>M</m> is Artinian, so is <m>N</m>. Therefore, <m>N</m> has a simple submodule, say <m>S</m> (see <xref ref="cor-artinian-module-has-simple-submodule"/>). By definition of socle, <m>S\leq\soc{M}</m> and hence, <m>N\cap\soc{M}\neq 0</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <corollary xml:id="cor-injectivity-for-socle-of-artinian-module">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>M</m> be a nonzero Artinian right <m>A</m>-module. If <m>f\colon M\to N</m> is an <m>A</m>-module homomorphism such that the restriction <m>f|_{\soc{M}}\colon\soc{M}\to N</m> is injective then, <m>f</m> is injective.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>
    <proof>
        <p>
            By <xref ref="lem-socle-of-artinian-module-is-essential"/>, <m>\soc{M}\neq 0</m> is an essential submodule of <m>M</m>. So, if <m>\ker f\neq 0</m> then, <m>\soc{M}\cap\ker f\neq 0</m>, a contradiction to the injectivity of <m>f|_{\soc{M}}</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>
</section>